{ignore}
<p><code>{icon}</code> writes an icon from PAD's own set (<code>lib/icon.php</code>): 79
interface symbols drawn on a 24 by 24 grid as lines, circles and rectangles, written as
inline SVG - no icon font, no sprite file, no request. The icon is drawn with
<code>stroke="currentColor"</code>, round caps and joins and no fill, so it takes the colour
of the text around it and scales with <code>size</code>.</p>

<p>Without <code>label</code> the icon is decoration - <code>aria-hidden="true"</code> and
<code>focusable="false"</code> - right for an icon beside the text it goes with, like
<code>{icon 'trash'} Delete</code>. An icon that stands alone in a button or a link needs
<code>label</code>: it then becomes <code>role="img"</code> with that name, so a screen
reader says what the button does.</p>

<p>Each icon has the classes <code>pad-icon pad-icon-&lt;name&gt;</code> for styling;
<code>stroke</code> makes the lines lighter or heavier. No name, or a name not in the set, is
an error under the strict check that names the icons close to it (<code>did you mean
arrow-left?</code>). <code>padIconNames ()</code> answers the whole list to PHP. For a
picture character in text see <a href="?tag/emoji">{emoji}</a>.</p>
{/ignore}
